1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,957
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:03,957 --> 00:00:08,190
Science, Unit 4, part two. Your programs
will learn to make decisions, repeat

3
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:12,422
steps, use functions, and store data in
lists. After every lesson, we'll work

4
00:00:12,422 --> 00:00:16,764
through a full example step by step, and
we'll finish by putting the whole unit

5
00:00:16,764 --> 00:00:17,259
together.

6
00:00:18,432 --> 00:00:23,680
Programs make decisions all the time. In
Python, decisions start with the word if.

7
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:25,346
Let's see how they work.

8
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:31,155
Here's an if statement. If temp is greater
than thirty, colon. Then, indented

9
00:00:31,155 --> 00:00:32,789
underneath, print it's hot.

10
00:00:33,239 --> 00:00:38,087
If the condition is True, the indented
lines run. If it's False, Python skips

11
00:00:38,087 --> 00:00:38,402
them.

12
00:00:39,552 --> 00:00:43,947
In Python, indentation matters. The
indented lines belong to the if.

13
00:00:44,397 --> 00:00:49,090
Use four spaces. Forget to indent, and
Python gives an indentation error.

14
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:51,669
Add else for a second path.

15
00:00:52,119 --> 00:00:57,214
If temp is greater than thirty, print hot.
Else, print not hot. Exactly one of the

16
00:00:57,214 --> 00:00:57,773
two runs.

17
00:00:58,923 --> 00:01:01,589
For more choices, use elif, short for else
if.

18
00:01:02,039 --> 00:01:06,903
Python checks each condition from the top,
and runs only the first one that's True.

19
00:01:07,353 --> 00:01:12,708
If score is at least ninety, grade A. elif
at least eighty, grade B. else, grade C.

20
00:01:13,858 --> 00:01:15,970
Let's trace it with a score of eighty
five.

21
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:21,220
At least ninety? No. At least eighty? Yes,
so the grade is B. The else is skipped.

22
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:27,409
Your turn. x is seven. If x is greater
than ten, print big. elif x is greater

23
00:01:27,409 --> 00:01:31,074
than five, print medium. else, print
small. What prints?

24
00:01:31,524 --> 00:01:32,719
Pause the video now.

25
00:01:33,169 --> 00:01:37,819
Seven isn't greater than ten, but it is
greater than five. So it prints medium.

26
00:01:38,269 --> 00:01:41,256
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

27
00:01:42,406 --> 00:01:46,267
Let's write an if, elif, else chain with
four paths, and test every one.

28
00:01:47,417 --> 00:01:52,681
The problem: a theater charges by age.
Under five is free. Under thirteen is

29
00:01:52,681 --> 00:01:58,084
eight dollars. Sixty five and over is nine
dollars. Everyone else pays twelve.

30
00:01:58,534 --> 00:02:01,905
Find the price for ages three, ten,
seventy and thirty.

31
00:02:03,055 --> 00:02:07,751
Here's the code. Python checks each
condition from the top, and runs only the

32
00:02:07,751 --> 00:02:08,665
first True one.

33
00:02:09,115 --> 00:02:12,401
Order matters: under five must come before
under thirteen.

34
00:02:13,551 --> 00:02:16,324
Age three: under five is True right away.
Free.

35
00:02:16,774 --> 00:02:20,315
Age ten: under five, no. Under thirteen,
yes. Eight dollars.

36
00:02:20,765 --> 00:02:25,011
Age seventy: no, no, then sixty five or
more, yes. Nine dollars.

37
00:02:25,461 --> 00:02:29,471
Age thirty: every condition is False, so
else runs. Twelve dollars.

38
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:32,733
Each age gets exactly one price.

39
00:02:33,183 --> 00:02:37,834
Common mistake: swapping the first two
checks. If under thirteen came first, a

40
00:02:37,834 --> 00:02:40,159
three year old would pay eight dollars.

41
00:02:41,309 --> 00:02:44,851
Your turn. Using the same code, what does
a thirteen year old pay?

42
00:02:45,301 --> 00:02:46,495
Pause the video now.

43
00:02:46,945 --> 00:02:51,786
Twelve dollars. Thirteen is not under
thirteen, and not sixty five or more, so

44
00:02:51,786 --> 00:02:52,407
else runs.

45
00:02:52,857 --> 00:02:53,923
Next, while loops.

46
00:02:55,073 --> 00:02:58,846
What if you want to print one to a
hundred? You don't write a hundred print

47
00:02:58,846 --> 00:02:59,148
lines.

48
00:02:59,598 --> 00:03:00,494
You write a loop.

49
00:03:01,644 --> 00:03:06,166
A while loop repeats its indented block as
long as its condition is True.

50
00:03:06,616 --> 00:03:11,344
Each time, Python checks the condition
first. When it becomes False, the loop

51
00:03:11,344 --> 00:03:11,651
ends.

52
00:03:12,801 --> 00:03:18,168
Here's a countdown. n equals three. While
n is greater than zero, print n, then n

53
00:03:18,168 --> 00:03:21,548
equals n minus one. After the loop, print
lift off.

54
00:03:21,998 --> 00:03:24,003
It prints three, two, one, then lift off.

55
00:03:25,153 --> 00:03:30,048
Every while loop needs three things: a
starting value, a condition, and a change

56
00:03:30,048 --> 00:03:31,638
that moves toward the end.

57
00:03:32,088 --> 00:03:37,032
Forget the change, and the condition stays
True forever. That's an infinite loop.

58
00:03:37,032 --> 00:03:38,680
Press control C to stop it.

59
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:43,222
While loops are great when you don't know
how many times to repeat.

60
00:03:43,672 --> 00:03:48,003
For example, keep asking for a password
until the user types the right one.

61
00:03:49,153 --> 00:03:52,246
Python also has break, which leaves a loop
right away.

62
00:03:52,696 --> 00:03:56,110
It's handy, but a clear condition is
usually easier to read.

63
00:03:57,260 --> 00:04:03,198
Your turn. total equals zero, i equals
one. While i is at most four, total plus

64
00:04:03,198 --> 00:04:06,732
equals i, and i plus equals one. What is
total?

65
00:04:07,182 --> 00:04:08,376
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:08,826 --> 00:04:12,069
One plus two plus three plus four. Total
is ten.

67
00:04:12,519 --> 00:04:15,506
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

68
00:04:16,656 --> 00:04:20,240
Let's use a while loop when we don't know
how many times to repeat.

69
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:25,537
The problem: you save fifteen dollars a
week, and want at least one hundred

70
00:04:25,537 --> 00:04:26,809
dollars for headphones.

71
00:04:27,259 --> 00:04:30,117
How many weeks will it take, and how much
will you have?

72
00:04:31,267 --> 00:04:33,678
Start the balance and the week count at
zero.

73
00:04:34,128 --> 00:04:38,075
While the balance is below a hundred, add
fifteen and count one more week.

74
00:04:38,525 --> 00:04:42,685
The change, adding fifteen, moves us
toward the end, so the loop stops.

75
00:04:43,835 --> 00:04:48,549
Week one: fifteen. Week two: thirty. Then
forty five, sixty, seventy five.

76
00:04:48,999 --> 00:04:53,266
Week six: ninety. Ninety is still under a
hundred, so the loop runs again.

77
00:04:53,716 --> 00:04:58,025
Week seven: one hundred five. Now the
condition is False, and the loop ends.

78
00:04:59,175 --> 00:05:02,503
It takes seven weeks, and you'll have one
hundred five dollars.

79
00:05:02,953 --> 00:05:07,634
Common mistake: forgetting the balance
line. Then balance stays zero, and the

80
00:05:07,634 --> 00:05:08,607
loop never ends.

81
00:05:09,757 --> 00:05:11,378
Your turn. What does this print?

82
00:05:11,828 --> 00:05:13,023
Pause the video now.

83
00:05:13,473 --> 00:05:15,478
Three. n goes ten, five, two, one.

84
00:05:15,928 --> 00:05:17,443
Next, for loops and range.

85
00:05:18,593 --> 00:05:21,942
When you know how many times to repeat, a
for loop is simpler.

86
00:05:22,392 --> 00:05:24,952
It pairs perfectly with a function called
range.

87
00:05:26,102 --> 00:05:28,534
for i in range of five, colon, print i.

88
00:05:28,984 --> 00:05:34,243
This prints zero, one, two, three, four.
Five numbers, starting at zero, stopping

89
00:05:34,243 --> 00:05:35,022
before five.

90
00:05:36,172 --> 00:05:41,798
range can take a start and a stop. range
of two comma six gives two, three, four,

91
00:05:41,798 --> 00:05:42,145
five.

92
00:05:42,595 --> 00:05:48,461
A third number is the step. range of zero
comma ten comma two gives zero, two, four,

93
00:05:48,461 --> 00:05:49,230
six, eight.

94
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:54,177
A for loop can also go through a string,
one character at a time.

95
00:05:54,627 --> 00:05:58,168
for letter in cat, print letter. It prints
c, then a, then t.

96
00:05:59,318 --> 00:06:01,238
A common pattern is adding things up.

97
00:06:01,688 --> 00:06:07,074
Start total at zero. For each number in
range one to six, add it to total. At the

98
00:06:07,074 --> 00:06:08,536
end, total is fifteen.

99
00:06:09,686 --> 00:06:11,158
So which loop should you use?

100
00:06:11,608 --> 00:06:16,175
for, when you know the count or have a
collection to go through. while, when you

101
00:06:16,175 --> 00:06:17,944
repeat until something happens.

102
00:06:19,094 --> 00:06:24,219
Your turn. What does this print? for i in
range of one comma ten comma three, print

103
00:06:24,219 --> 00:06:24,342
i.

104
00:06:24,792 --> 00:06:25,987
Pause the video now.

105
00:06:26,437 --> 00:06:31,344
Start at one, step by three, stop before
ten. It prints one, four, seven.

106
00:06:31,794 --> 00:06:34,780
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

107
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:40,239
Let's use a for loop with range and an
accumulator, and trace every pass.

108
00:06:41,389 --> 00:06:46,636
The problem: add up the squares of one to
five. That's one plus four plus nine plus

109
00:06:46,636 --> 00:06:48,216
sixteen plus twenty five.

110
00:06:49,366 --> 00:06:54,890
Start total at zero. range of one comma
six gives one to five, because it stops

111
00:06:54,890 --> 00:06:55,659
before six.

112
00:06:56,109 --> 00:06:58,456
Each pass, add i times i to the total.

113
00:06:59,606 --> 00:07:03,638
i is one: total becomes one. i is two:
plus four, total five.

114
00:07:04,088 --> 00:07:08,269
i is three: plus nine, fourteen. i is
four: plus sixteen, thirty.

115
00:07:08,719 --> 00:07:13,754
i is five: plus twenty five, fifty five.
range is used up, so the loop ends.

116
00:07:14,904 --> 00:07:16,653
The program prints fifty five.

117
00:07:17,103 --> 00:07:22,079
Common mistake: range of one comma five.
That stops before five, so you'd miss

118
00:07:22,079 --> 00:07:23,802
twenty five and get thirty.

119
00:07:24,952 --> 00:07:29,133
Your turn. What does list of range of
three comma twelve comma three give?

120
00:07:29,583 --> 00:07:30,778
Pause the video now.

121
00:07:31,228 --> 00:07:34,705
Three, six, nine. Twelve is the stop, so
it's not included.

122
00:07:35,155 --> 00:07:36,307
Next, functions.

123
00:07:37,457 --> 00:07:40,934
You've used functions like print and len.
Now you'll make your own.

124
00:07:41,384 --> 00:07:45,246
A function is a named block of code you
can run whenever you want.

125
00:07:46,396 --> 00:07:51,945
To define a function, write def, a name,
parentheses, and a colon. Then indent the

126
00:07:51,945 --> 00:07:52,284
body.

127
00:07:52,734 --> 00:07:55,294
def greet, colon, print hello, print
welcome.

128
00:07:56,444 --> 00:08:00,390
Defining a function doesn't run it. It
just teaches Python the steps.

129
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:03,848
To run it, call it by name with
parentheses: greet.

130
00:08:04,998 --> 00:08:07,772
You can call a function as many times as
you like.

131
00:08:08,222 --> 00:08:11,571
Call greet three times, and the two lines
print three times.

132
00:08:12,721 --> 00:08:17,350
Functions keep code organized. Each one
does one job, with a clear name.

133
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:20,830
And if you need to fix something, you fix
it in one place.

134
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:26,844
Python reads a file from top to bottom, so
define a function before you call it.

135
00:08:27,294 --> 00:08:29,576
Call it too early, and you'll get a name
error.

136
00:08:30,726 --> 00:08:35,471
Your turn. def beep, colon, print beep.
Then the program calls beep twice. How

137
00:08:35,471 --> 00:08:36,870
many lines are printed?

138
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:38,515
Pause the video now.

139
00:08:38,965 --> 00:08:43,168
Two. The definition doesn't print
anything, and each call prints one line.

140
00:08:43,618 --> 00:08:46,604
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

141
00:08:47,755 --> 00:08:52,662
Let's follow a program where one function
calls another, and count the output lines.

142
00:08:53,812 --> 00:08:58,097
The problem: print a birthday card with a
line of dashes above and below the

143
00:08:58,097 --> 00:08:58,548
message.

144
00:08:58,998 --> 00:09:02,560
Use a function for the dashes, and a
function for the whole card.

145
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:09,001
line prints ten dashes. card calls line,
prints the message, and calls line again.

146
00:09:09,451 --> 00:09:13,789
Defining them does nothing yet. The last
line calls card, and that starts

147
00:09:13,789 --> 00:09:14,443
everything.

148
00:09:15,593 --> 00:09:18,622
Python reads both definitions, then
reaches card.

149
00:09:19,072 --> 00:09:22,934
card calls line: dashes. Back in card, it
prints happy birthday.

150
00:09:23,384 --> 00:09:27,949
card calls line again: dashes. card
finishes, and so does the program.

151
00:09:29,099 --> 00:09:30,486
The card is three lines.

152
00:09:30,936 --> 00:09:35,894
If you want two cards, just call card
twice. And if you want stars instead of

153
00:09:35,894 --> 00:09:38,018
dashes, you change only one line.

154
00:09:39,168 --> 00:09:43,286
Your turn. If the program ends with card,
card, how many lines print?

155
00:09:43,736 --> 00:09:44,930
Pause the video now.

156
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:47,386
Six. Each card prints three lines.

157
00:09:47,836 --> 00:09:49,500
Next, parameters and return.

158
00:09:50,649 --> 00:09:54,446
A function that does the same thing every
time is a little limited.

159
00:09:54,896 --> 00:09:59,248
Parameters let you send values in, and
return lets you get a value back.

160
00:10:00,398 --> 00:10:03,620
A parameter is a variable in the
function's parentheses.

161
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:09,719
def greet, name, colon, print hello plus
name. Now greet of Sam prints hello Sam,

162
00:10:09,719 --> 00:10:12,091
and greet of Ana prints hello Ana.

163
00:10:12,541 --> 00:10:15,720
The value you pass in, like Sam, is called
an argument.

164
00:10:16,870 --> 00:10:19,110
return sends a value back to the caller.

165
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:26,049
def square, x, colon, return x times x.
Then y equals square of four makes y equal

166
00:10:26,049 --> 00:10:26,920
to sixteen.

167
00:10:28,070 --> 00:10:32,942
Print and return are different. Print
shows a value on the screen. Return hands

168
00:10:32,942 --> 00:10:35,408
a value back, so the program can use it.

169
00:10:35,858 --> 00:10:40,488
A function that prints but doesn't return
gives back a special value called None.

170
00:10:41,638 --> 00:10:43,792
Functions can take several parameters.

171
00:10:44,242 --> 00:10:49,618
def area, width comma height, return width
times height. area of three comma five is

172
00:10:49,618 --> 00:10:50,130
fifteen.

173
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:54,118
When a function hits return, it stops
right there.

174
00:10:54,568 --> 00:10:56,658
Any lines after the return don't run.

175
00:10:57,808 --> 00:11:02,608
Your turn. def double, n, return n times
two. What is double of double of three?

176
00:11:03,058 --> 00:11:04,253
Pause the video now.

177
00:11:04,703 --> 00:11:08,308
The inside call gives six. The outside
call gives twelve.

178
00:11:08,758 --> 00:11:11,745
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

179
00:11:12,895 --> 00:11:17,251
Let's write a function that takes a
parameter and returns an answer, then use

180
00:11:17,251 --> 00:11:17,930
that answer.

181
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:24,517
The problem: write a function that turns
Celsius into Fahrenheit. The formula is C

182
00:11:24,517 --> 00:11:27,037
times nine over five, plus thirty two.

183
00:11:27,487 --> 00:11:29,684
Then use it to decide if a day is hot.

184
00:11:30,834 --> 00:11:33,672
c is the parameter. return sends the
answer back.

185
00:11:34,122 --> 00:11:39,303
f equals to f of twenty five stores the
returned value. Then we can test it with

186
00:11:39,303 --> 00:11:39,498
if.

187
00:11:40,648 --> 00:11:44,530
Calling to f of zero: c is zero, the
result is thirty two.

188
00:11:44,980 --> 00:11:49,119
to f of twenty five: c is twenty five, the
result is seventy seven.

189
00:11:49,569 --> 00:11:54,932
to f of one hundred: two hundred twelve.
And to f of thirty is eighty six, which is

190
00:11:54,932 --> 00:11:56,289
over eighty: hot day.

191
00:11:57,439 --> 00:12:01,002
The program prints seventy seven point
zero, then hot day.

192
00:12:01,452 --> 00:12:05,639
Common mistake: printing inside the
function instead of returning. Then f

193
00:12:05,639 --> 00:12:08,278
would be None, and the if couldn't compare
it.

194
00:12:09,428 --> 00:12:13,780
Your turn. What does this print? Work from
the inside call to the outside one.

195
00:12:14,230 --> 00:12:15,425
Pause the video now.

196
00:12:15,875 --> 00:12:19,182
Twelve. double of three is six, and double
of six is twelve.

197
00:12:19,632 --> 00:12:20,506
Next, lists.

198
00:12:21,657 --> 00:12:26,499
What if you need to store a whole class of
test scores? One variable per score would

199
00:12:26,499 --> 00:12:27,076
be a mess.

200
00:12:27,526 --> 00:12:28,934
Python has lists for that.

201
00:12:30,084 --> 00:12:35,289
A list holds many values in order, inside
square brackets, separated by commas.

202
00:12:35,739 --> 00:12:38,427
scores equals ninety, seventy five, eighty
eight.

203
00:12:39,577 --> 00:12:44,313
Lists use indexes, just like strings. The
first item is at index zero.

204
00:12:44,763 --> 00:12:50,672
scores, bracket zero, is ninety. Negative
one gives the last item. len of scores is

205
00:12:50,672 --> 00:12:51,099
three.

206
00:12:52,249 --> 00:12:54,084
Unlike strings, lists can change.

207
00:12:54,534 --> 00:12:59,913
scores, bracket one, equals eighty
replaces an item. append adds an item to

208
00:12:59,913 --> 00:13:02,064
the end. remove takes one out.

209
00:13:03,214 --> 00:13:05,561
A for loop goes through every item in a
list.

210
00:13:06,011 --> 00:13:11,238
for s in scores, add s to total. Divide by
len of scores, and you have the average.

211
00:13:12,388 --> 00:13:15,801
Lists can hold any type: numbers, strings,
even other lists.

212
00:13:16,251 --> 00:13:19,088
They're one of the most useful tools in
all of Python.

213
00:13:20,238 --> 00:13:25,370
Your turn. nums equals four, eight,
fifteen. nums dot append sixteen. What is

214
00:13:25,370 --> 00:13:28,836
len of nums, and what is nums, bracket
negative one?

215
00:13:29,286 --> 00:13:30,480
Pause the video now.

216
00:13:30,930 --> 00:13:35,432
The list now has four items, so len is
four. The last item is sixteen.

217
00:13:35,882 --> 00:13:38,868
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

218
00:13:40,018 --> 00:13:44,537
Let's loop through a list to find its
biggest item, a pattern you'll use again

219
00:13:44,537 --> 00:13:45,117
and again.

220
00:13:46,267 --> 00:13:51,515
The problem: test scores are seventy two,
ninety five, eighty eight and sixty one.

221
00:13:51,515 --> 00:13:53,883
Find the top score without using max.

222
00:13:55,033 --> 00:13:58,190
Start best at the first item. Then check
every score.

223
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:01,200
If a score beats best, it becomes the new
best.

224
00:14:02,350 --> 00:14:05,699
best starts at seventy two. s is seventy
two: not bigger.

225
00:14:06,149 --> 00:14:09,307
s is ninety five: bigger, so best is
ninety five.

226
00:14:09,757 --> 00:14:13,298
s is eighty eight: not bigger. s is sixty
one: not bigger.

227
00:14:14,448 --> 00:14:16,176
The program prints ninety five.

228
00:14:16,626 --> 00:14:21,624
Why start best at scores, bracket zero,
and not at zero? If every score were

229
00:14:21,624 --> 00:14:23,794
negative, zero would wrongly win.

230
00:14:24,944 --> 00:14:26,565
Your turn. What does this print?

231
00:14:27,015 --> 00:14:28,210
Pause the video now.

232
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:31,519
Four and sixteen. append adds sixteen to
the end.

233
00:14:31,969 --> 00:14:33,953
Next, let's put the whole unit together.

234
00:14:35,103 --> 00:14:39,199
You've learned decisions, while loops, for
loops, functions, return and lists.

235
00:14:39,649 --> 00:14:43,937
Now let's build one complete Python
program that uses every one of them.

236
00:14:45,087 --> 00:14:47,028
Here's the plan: a class grade book.

237
00:14:47,478 --> 00:14:52,109
It reads scores until the teacher types
done, prints each student's letter grade,

238
00:14:52,109 --> 00:14:53,366
and the class average.

239
00:14:54,516 --> 00:14:59,147
Part one: a function called letter. It
takes a score, checks the highest grade

240
00:14:59,147 --> 00:15:00,810
first, and returns a letter.

241
00:15:01,260 --> 00:15:03,478
Each return ends the function right away.

242
00:15:04,628 --> 00:15:09,006
Part two: average takes a list. It adds
every item with a for loop, the

243
00:15:09,006 --> 00:15:10,239
accumulator pattern.

244
00:15:10,689 --> 00:15:13,334
Then it returns the total divided by the
length.

245
00:15:14,484 --> 00:15:17,535
Part three: start with an empty list. Ask
for a score.

246
00:15:17,985 --> 00:15:22,694
While the entry isn't done, convert it to
an int, append it, and ask again. We don't

247
00:15:22,694 --> 00:15:26,113
know how many students there are, so while
is the right loop.

248
00:15:27,263 --> 00:15:31,885
Part four: loop over the indexes with
range of len. Print the student number,

249
00:15:31,885 --> 00:15:33,386
the score and its letter.

250
00:15:33,836 --> 00:15:36,822
Then call average, and reuse letter for
the class grade.

251
00:15:37,972 --> 00:15:42,512
Let's run it. The teacher types ninety
two, seventy eight, eighty five, sixty

252
00:15:42,512 --> 00:15:43,455
four, then done.

253
00:15:43,905 --> 00:15:48,470
Each student gets a letter, and the class
average is seventy nine point eight, a C.

254
00:15:49,620 --> 00:15:54,312
Your turn. The teacher types one hundred,
eighty, then done. What are the last two

255
00:15:54,312 --> 00:15:54,655
lines?

256
00:15:55,105 --> 00:15:56,300
Pause the video now.

257
00:15:56,750 --> 00:16:00,910
Student two, eighty, B. Then class average
ninety point zero, A.

258
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:05,413
That's the end of Unit 4. Next, Unit 5:
how the Internet works.

259
00:16:06,563 --> 00:16:10,992
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

260
00:16:10,992 --> 00:16:11,384
lesson.

261
00:16:11,834 --> 00:16:12,666
See you there.

